    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Coloman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> was crowned King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> at Biograd in 1102, and the union with Hungary lasted, in changing forms, until 1918.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> kept institutions of its own within the union: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-parliament">Sabor</a>, an assembly of nobles, the ban as the king's governor, and a customary law distinct from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s.</li><li>The Ottoman wars of the 15th and 16th centuries, from the defeat at Krbava Field in 1493 to Mohács in 1526, reduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>'s territory and left it weak and divided.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Pacta Conventa, the supposed agreement between Coloman and twelve Croatian noble tribes, survives only in a 14th-century transcript, and most modern Croatian historians regard it as a forgery.</p></aside>
    </section>

