    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-zvonimir">Demetrius Zvonimir</a>, of the Svetoslavić branch of the House of Trpimirović, had married Helen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, sister of King Ladislaus I. After Zvonimir died in 1089 and his successor Stephen II died without an heir in 1091, civil war broke out. Nobles around Helen offered the throne to Ladislaus I, who conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> in 1091 but was halted near Mount Gvozd and forced to withdraw by a Cuman attack on Hungary.</p><p>His nephew Coloman continued the campaign and in 1097 defeated King Petar, who was killed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gvozd-mountain">Battle of Gvozd Mountain</a>. Negotiations followed with the Croatian lords, and in 1102 Coloman was crowned in Biograd. The Pacta Conventa describes twelve noble tribes recognising him in return for keeping their lands and tax exemption; it is not an authentic document of 1102, but some agreement almost certainly existed. Historians long disputed whether the union was a personal union or an annexation; Hungarian legal historians today see it as most like a personal union.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the 12th century a layer of powerful noble families formed, mostly descendants of the original twelve noble tribes. They held whole counties and presided over local courts, so the local population was entirely separated from any state organisation. The leading families were the Šubić of Bribir, the Babonić in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a>, the Kačić of Omiš, known for piracy, and the Frankopan, Princes of Krk. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> gained property in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>.</p><p>Kings sought to balance the nobles by making towns free royal cities: Varaždin in 1220, Vukovar in 1231, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virovitica">Virovitica</a> in 1234, Gradec in 1242, Križevci in 1252 and others. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">Free cities</a> elected their own councils and courts, collected their own taxes and managed their own trade. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zadar">Treaty of Zadar</a> of 1358 the economy flourished, especially in the coastal cities, new royal towns grew on trade routes, and richer merchants came to dominate the towns.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hungarians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> for the Dalmatian coastal cities. In 1116 Venice defeated Ban Cledin and seized Biograd, Split, Trogir, Šibenik and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zadar</a>; the cities changed hands repeatedly, and in 1202 Venice and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> sacked Zadar. In 1242 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a>, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> and attacked Klis, where they failed to take the fortress; afterwards fortresses such as Medvedgrad were built and nobles were allowed to build castles.</p><p>After 1463 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> regularly. Count Petar Zrinski won the first major victory near Glina in 1478, and in 1483 Ban Matthias Geréb and the Frankopans defeated about 7,000 akıncı at the Una River. On 9 September 1493 a Croatian army of about 10,000 suffered a huge defeat at Krbava Field. Ban Petar Berislavić won at Dubica in 1513 but was killed in 1520, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knin">Knin</a> fell on 29 May 1522, and Petar Kružić defended Klis for almost 25 years.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> was ruled for the king by a governor, the ban. From 1198 Croatia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> were held by Dukes of Croatia, who ran their duchy as semi-independent rulers with a ban beneath them. In 1225 the territory was divided between a Ban of Croatia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and a Ban of Slavonia; the offices were often held by one person after 1345 and merged by 1476. The country was divided into counties (županije) under counts who were local hereditary nobles ruling by Croatian customary law. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-parliament">Sabor</a>, an assembly of nobles, and the ban preserved institutions of separate statehood.</p><p>Croatian custom, the consuetudo Croatorum, differed from Hungarian law; in 1361 Louis I ordered Ban Nicholas Szécsi to judge by Hungarian custom instead. In Church affairs, Croatia south of Gvozd was under the Archbishop of Split, and Slavonia under the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalocsa">Kalocsa</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom's legal and noble institutions, and the continuous mention of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> in official documents, preserved a separate pre-modern Croatian nation within the union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Its customary law was later kept by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and codified in statutes such as those of Vinodol and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poljica">Poljica</a>.</p><p>Powerful nobles at times ruled almost alone: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-i-subic-of-bribir">Paul I Šubić of Bribir</a>, ban from 1274, became hereditary ban in the succession war of the 1290s, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> in 1299, issued his own money and helped bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles I of Anjou</a> to the throne. In 1519 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a> called Croatia the forefront of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> (Antemurale Christianitatis) for its soldiers' part in the wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. Three crowned lion heads represented the kingdom from the 13th century, and the checkerboard became the official arms after 1527.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Royal authority was repeatedly undermined by the nobles. After Paul Šubić's death in 1312 his family declined, and Ivan Nelipić ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knin">Knin</a> outside the king's authority until 1344. The anti-court movement after 1382 brought the capture of Queen Mary and her mother Elizabeth, Elizabeth's murder in 1387 and the Bloody <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-parliament">Sabor</a> of Križevci in 1397. In 1409 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-of-naples">Ladislaus of Naples</a> sold his rights in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> for 100,000 ducats.</p><p>The Ottoman advance after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> in 1463 reduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>'s territory and left it weak and divided. On 29 August 1526 the Hungarian army was routed at Mohács and Louis II was killed. With two rival kings elected in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, the Croatian Sabor unanimously elected Ferdinand of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a> as King of Croatia at Cetin on 1 January 1527, while the Slavonian nobility sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-zapolya">John Zápolya</a>. Both crowns passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and the realms became part of the Habsburg monarchy.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>From 1102 to 1526 the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> shared its king with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> while keeping its own ban, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-parliament">Sabor</a>, counties and customary law. Its history was one of struggle with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> for Dalmatia, powerful noble houses such as the Šubić, the Mongol invasion and the Ottoman wars, ending with Mohács and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> election at Cetin.</em></p>

